From 4f1166b8b82173b0afa12696ed093d3425360b66 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dimitry B <dimitryB@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 01:04:22 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 12/15] eip155: validate EIP-6492 signatures first; ignore a
 wrapper around the payer's own signature

EIP-6492-wrapped signatures skipped the signature-first rule of 0003: the
wrapped signature was only checked by the validator inside the transfer
simulation, after balance and allowance. An unfunded payer with a tampered
inner signature got insufficient_funds / 412 permit2_allowance_required, a
funded one got invalid_transaction_state ("Multicall3: call failed"), instead
of invalid_exact_evm_payload_signature.

Worse, /settle did not validate the signature at all before sending. For a
payer without code it sent Multicall3.aggregate3([factory.call(<calldata from
the payload>), transfer]): a call to a caller-chosen address with caller-chosen
calldata, paid for by the facilitator, even when /verify had rejected the
payload.

- assert_signed_by_payer, which the v2 exact verify and settle paths of both
  methods run before balances, allowances and simulation, now asks the
  EIP-6492 validator (isValidSigWithSideEffects, as an eth_call: nothing is
  deployed for real) about a wrapped signature. A rejection or a revert is an
  invalid signature; only a failure to reach the chain is reported as such. A
  settle therefore never sends the factory call for a signature the validator
  rejects.
- StructuredSignature::try_from_bytes treats an EIP-6492 wrapper around a
  64/65-byte signature that recovers to the payer as the plain EOA signature:
  the payer's own key signed, so no wallet needs deploying and the factory is
  never called.

The deployment path itself is unchanged: Multicall3 calls the factory with
allowFailure, then the transfer, so msg.sender of both calls is Multicall3 and
the factory call cannot spend anything the facilitator holds; gas is its only
cost.
